{
  "dom_id": "step-q15688",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14221452/assets/3bde5b93aa28654977fdb55b0b2808fde2fb2f54ba33a6e9a2a4281b6966682f.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx={t}^{4}-2{t}^{2},\\,y={t}^{3}-1\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in [1,\\sqrt{2}]?$",
  "worked_solution": "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}-2{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}-4t \\\\  & =4t({t}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}({t}^{3}-1)4t({t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}t({t}^{3}-1)({t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a positive number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\ge 0$ for all $t\\in [1,\\sqrt{2}].$\n\nTherefore, the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=4{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}t({t}^{3}-1)({t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [
    {
      "key": "selection",
      "type": "radio",
      "choices": [
